.TH epm 1 "ESP Package Manager" "20 November 2020" "Jim Jagielski"
.SH NAME
epm \- create software packages.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B epm
[
.B \-a
.I architecture
] [
.B \-f
.I format
] [
.B \-g
] [
.B \-k
] [
.B \-m
.I name
] [
\fB\-n\fR[\fBmrs\fR] ] [
.B \-s
.I setup.ext
] [
.B \-\-depend
] [
.B \-\-help
] [
.B \-\-keep\-files
] [
.B \-\-output\-dir
.I directory
] [
.B \-\-setup\-image
.I setup.ext
] [
.B \-\-setup\-program
.I /foo/bar/setup
] [
.B \-\-setup\-types
.I setup.types
] [
.B \-\-uninstall\-program
.I /foo/bar/uninst
] [
.B \-v
] [
.I name=value
\...
.I name=value
]
.I product
[
.I listfile
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR epm (1)
generates software packages complete with installation, removal, and (if necessary) patch scripts.
Unless otherwise specified, the files required for \fIproduct\fR are read from a file named "\fIproduct\fR.list".
.SH OPTIONS
The following options are recognized:
.TP 5
\fB\-a \fIarchitecture\fR
Specifies the actual architecture for the software.
Without this option the generic processor architecture is used ("intel", "sparc", "mips", etc.)
.TP 5
\fB\-f bsd\fR
Generate a BSD distribution suitable for installation on a FreeBSD, NetBSD, or OpenBSD system.
.TP 5
\fB\-f deb\fR
Generate a Debian distribution suitable for installation on a Debian-based Linux system.
.TP 5
\fB\-f native\fR
Generate a native distribution.
This uses \fIdeb\fR or \fIrpm\fR for Linux, \fIbsd\fR for FreeBSD, NetBSD, and OpenBSD, and \fImacos\fR for macOS.
All other operating systems default to the \fIportable\fR format.
.TP 5
\fB\-f macos\fR
.TP 5
\fB\-f macos\-signed\fR
Generate a macOS software package.
The output is a product archive built with productbuild from one component package per subpackage, wrapped in a disk image.
Installer shows the %license, %readme and %description of the list file, and offers each %subpackage as an install choice named by its first %description line.
Application bundles in the payload are installed at the path given in the list file rather than relocated to an existing copy.
Initialization scripts get both a StartupItem and a launchd job, so they start on every macOS release.
When \fBepm\fR is not run as root the owners and groups in the list file cannot be applied and pkgbuild's recommended ownership is used instead.
The \fImacos\-signed\fR format signs the product archive with the installer identity in the EPM_SIGNING_IDENTITY environment variable and checks it with pkgutil.
If EPM_APPLICATION_IDENTITY is also set, the Mach\-O files and bundles in the package payload and the resulting disk image are signed with that application identity, and both the package and the disk image are notarized and stapled when EPM_NOTARY_KEYCHAIN_PROFILE is set.
Bundles are signed inside\-out: every Mach\-O file inside a bundle is signed first, nested bundles are sealed next, and the enclosing bundle last, so notarization sees a signature on every code object.
Each signed bundle, file and disk image is then checked with "codesign \-\-verify \-\-strict".
.TP 5
\fB\-f macos\-app\fR
Generate a macOS drag\-install disk image.
Every file in the list must live under /Applications; the image holds those application bundles, a link to /Applications to drag them onto, and the %license as an agreement shown before the image mounts.
When EPM_APPLICATION_IDENTITY is set the bundles are signed as for \fImacos\-signed\fR, each application is notarized and stapled before it goes on the image when EPM_NOTARY_KEYCHAIN_PROFILE is set, and the image itself is then signed and notarized.
.TP 5
\fB\-f portable\fR
Generate a portable distribution based on shell scripts and tar files.
The resulting distribution is installed and removed the same way on all operating systems. [default]
.TP 5
\fB\-f rpm\fR
.TP 5
\fB\-f rpm\-signed\fR
Generate a Red Hat Package Manager ("RPM") distribution suitable for installation on an RPM-based Linux system.
The \fIrpm\-signed\fR format uses the GPG private key you have defined in the ~/.rpmmacros file.
.TP 5
\fB\-g\fR
Disable stripping of executable files in the distribution.
.TP 5
\fB\-k\fR
Keep intermediate (spec, etc.) files used to create the distribution in the distribution directory.
.TP 5
\fB\-m \fIname\fR
Specifies the platform name as a string.
The default is to use the auto-generated name from the \fI\-n\fR option.
.TP 5
\fB\-n\fR[\fImrs\fR]
Specifies the operating system and machine information that is included in the package name.
Distributions normally are named "product-version-system-release-machine.ext" and "product-version-system-release-machine-patch.ext" for patch distributions.
The "system-release-machine" information can be customized or eliminated using the appropriate trailing letters.
Using \fI-n\fR by itself will remove the "system-release-machine" string from the filename entirely.
The letter 'm' includes the architecture (machine).
The letter 'r' includes the operating system version (release).
The letter 's' includes the operating system name.
.TP 5
\fB\-v\fR
Increases the amount of information that is reported.
Use multiple v's for more verbose output.
.TP 5
\fB\-\-depend\fR
Lists the dependent (source) files for all files in the package.
.TP 5
\fB\-\-output\-dir \fIdirectory\fR
Specifies the directory for output files.
The default directory is based on the operating system, version, and architecture.
.TP 5
\fB\-s \fIsetup.ext\fR
.TP 5
\fB\-\-setup\-image \fIsetup.ext\fR
Include the ESP Software Wizard with the specified image file with the distribution.
This option is currently only supported by portable distributions.
.TP 5
\fB\-\-setup\-program \fI/foo/bar/setup\fR
Specifies the setup executable to use with the distribution.
This option is currently only supported by portable distributions.
.TP 5
\fB\-\-setup\-types \fIsetup.types\fR
.LP
Specifies the \fIsetup.types\fR file to include with the distribution.
This option is currently only supported by portable distributions.
.TP 5
\fB\-\-uninstall\-program \fI/foo/bar/uninst\fR
Specifies the uninst executable to use with the distribution.
This option is currently only supported by portable distributions.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The following environment variables are supported by \fBepm\fR:
.TP 5
.B EPM_SIGNING_IDENTITY
The common name that should be used when signing a package.
For the \fImacos\-signed\fR format this is an installer identity, typically "Developer ID Installer", and it defaults to that value.
.TP 5
.B EPM_APPLICATION_IDENTITY
The common name of the application identity, typically "Developer ID Application", used to sign executables and disk images.
For the \fImacos\-signed\fR and \fImacos\-app\fR formats, setting this enables signing of the Mach\-O files and bundles in the payload and of the resulting disk image.
Executables are signed with the hardened runtime, which is required for notarization.
The value "\-" selects the ad\-hoc identity: the payload is signed without a certificate or timestamp, and the package and disk image are left unsigned, which is enough for a local build.
When this variable is unset no application signing is performed and \fImacos\-signed\fR prints a warning.
.TP 5
.B EPM_SIGNING_ENTITLEMENTS
The path of an entitlements property list to apply when signing executables with EPM_APPLICATION_IDENTITY.
Entitlements are applied to executables and application bundles only; libraries, frameworks and plug\-ins run under their host's.
A list file entry can name its own with the "entitlements(\fIfile\fR)" option, see \fIepm.list(5)\fR.
.TP 5
.B EPM_SIGNING_HARDENED_RUNTIME
Set to "no" to sign without the hardened runtime.
Notarization requires the hardened runtime, so this only makes sense for local builds.
.TP 5
.B EPM_SIGNING_KEYCHAIN
The path of the keychain holding the signing identities, passed to codesign and pkgbuild as \-\-keychain.
When unset the keychain search list is used.
.TP 5
.B EPM_NOTARY_KEYCHAIN_PROFILE
The name of a keychain profile created with "xcrun notarytool store\-credentials".
When set together with EPM_APPLICATION_IDENTITY, the \fImacos\-signed\fR format submits the product package and then the disk image to the Apple notary service and staples the resulting tickets, and the \fImacos\-app\fR format does the same for each application and the image.
A rejected submission fails the build and prints the notary log.
.TP 5
.B EPM_MACOS_IDENTIFIER
The reverse\-DNS package identifier, for example "org.example.product", that macOS records in its receipts.
Subpackages append their name to it.
It defaults to the product name, and is usually set in the list file with "$EPM_MACOS_IDENTIFIER=...".
.SH LIST FILES
The EPM list file format is now described in the \fIepm.list(5)\fR
man page.
